    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>There was no single Book of the Dead: each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> held its own selection of spells, and a fixed order appeared only with the Saite recension of the 25th and 26th Dynasties.</li><li>Its spells set out Egyptian beliefs about death: preserving the body, heart, name, ba and shadow, passing the guardians of the Duat and being judged before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>.</li><li>Named by Karl Richard Lepsius in 1842, it remains a vital source for Egyptian ideas of the afterlife and is studied today by the Book of the Dead Project in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many copies were prefabricated in workshops with spaces left for the owner's name; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> of Ani the name &quot;Ani&quot; is in a different hand from the rest and is in places mis-spelt or left out.</p></aside>
    </section>

